tion fails on some frames (poseLandmarks is null), those frames are skipped, which could cause gaps in the landmark sequence that the shot detector isn't aware of.","suggestion":"Consider tracking which frames had successful detections and either interpolating missing frames or adjusting the shot detector's frame range expectations accordingly."},{"severity":"minor","file":"src/analyzer.test.ts","line":701,"issue":"Duplicate helper function: createTrackingFrameProvider is defined twice - once inside a describe block (line 563) and once at module scope (line 701). The module-scope version shadows the inner one for some tests.","suggestion":"Remove the duplicate module-scope helper function or move all tests to use a single shared helper."},{"severity":"minor","file":"src/analyzer.ts","line":423,"issue":"Magic number 2 in landmark threshold check: The comment says 'empty video or no landmarks' but the check allPoseLandmarks.length < 2 is related to shot detector requirements. This could be extracted as a named constant for clarity.","suggestion":"Extract to a constant like MIN_FRAMES_FOR_SHOT_DETECTION = 2 with a comment explaining it matches the shot detector's minimum requirement."}],"summary":"The Video Analysis Pipeline implementation correctly fulfills the acceptance criteria. The analyzeVideo() method properly processes frames through pose detection, detects shots using the shot detector, extracts metrics for each shot via the metric orchestrator, and returns a complete AnalysisResult with video metadata. Edge cases for empty videos, no shots detected, and long videos are handled appropriately. Tests cover basic functionality, frame processing, shot detection integration, and metric extraction. However, there's a potential frame index alignment issue when some frames fail pose detection - the shot detector operates on filtered landmarks but returns indices as if all frames succeeded. This edge case should be monitored but isn't blocking as the current mock tests