,"file":"server.ts","line":191,"issue":"JSON.parse errors are not explicitly caught","suggestion":"Invalid JSON in the request body will throw an error that's caught by the outer try/catch, but the error message may be confusing to users. Consider wrapping JSON.parse in a try/catch with a clearer error message like 'Invalid JSON in request body'."},{"severity":"minor","file":"validate-metrics.html","line":1552,"issue":"Pose data depends on result.poseHistory which may not exist in the analyzer output","suggestion":"The code correctly handles the case when poseHistory doesn't exist by setting poseData to null. However, the debug log at line 1570 says 'No raw pose history available' which accurately reflects this. The Export Poses button will be disabled, which is correct behavior. Consider adding user-facing feedback about why poses aren't available (e.g., a tooltip on the disabled button)."},{"severity":"minor","file":"validate-metrics.html","line":957,"issue":"Export Poses button has a title attribute explaining it's disabled, but Export Labels doesn't clarify when it can be used","suggestion":"Add a more descriptive title to the Export Labels button similar to Export Poses, e.g., 'Export shot labels and orientation to server'"}],"summary":"The Server API & Export implementation correctly fulfills all acceptance criteria: POST /api/save-poses and POST /api/save-labels endpoints save data to test-data/<video-name>/ directories, video names are sanitized (spaces to hyphens, lowercase, special chars removed), Export Poses button is disabled until pose data is available, Export Labels validates complete shots before exporting, and success/error toast notifications are shown. The edge cases are handled: directories are created if they don't exist (fs.mkdir with recursive:true), files are overwritten without confirmation (simple overwrite behavior), network errors show error messages via catch blocks, and no poses disables the button. The tests in src/server-utils.test.t